{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LHzBLoRkMs5mckPdqp51k4","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Score: 4/6 (Weak Accept), Confidence: 4/5**\n\n**Main strengths identified:**\n- Well-operationalized double-bind framing with the RC-AUC metric\n- Careful confound control in direction extraction (L0 near-random, mid-layer emergence, cross-evidence robustness)\n- Comprehensive ablation structure that cleanly isolates each reward component\n- Strong statistical methodology throughout (bootstrap tests, 5-fold CV, CIs)\n- Novel R1-R3 disagreement and turn-depth analyses providing independently interesting findings\n\n**Main weaknesses identified:**\n1. Single-model full training (Qwen3-8B only) is insufficient for a methods paper; cross-model direction existence on Llama-3-8B does not substitute for a full training run\n2. Reward weights (0.3/0.2/0.5) are unexplained with no sensitivity analysis, despite the demonstrated non-additive component interactions\n3. GPT-4-generated contrastive pairs introduce potential bias; filtering pipeline is undocumented\n4. \"Masked sycophancy\" (Claim 3) risks circularity: negative d_syc projection defines sycophancy, so detecting it via d_syc is tautological; the 50-case human audit is thin\n5. Direction stability may be an artifact of LoRA's low-rank constraint rather than an intrinsic property\n\n**Key missing experiments:** full DC-GRPO on a second model family, reward weight sensitivity analysis, OOD sycophancy evaluation, continuous nonlinear probe comparison, calibration data size sensitivity on downstream performance.\n\nThe review was saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515-3/projects/contrastive_entrain_calib/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260518T110318/best/review_report.md`."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a22517085649a7b41 (use SendMessage with to: 'a22517085649a7b41' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 84308\ntool_uses: 13\nduration_ms: 112378</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"07a20ac4-8113-41af-ad97-9b49cb4c4cd6","uuid":"5abc3f71-c70c-416c-8b3b-366fe95b756f","timestamp":"2026-05-18T11:38:22.017Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a blind peer review of an ML paper. Your focus areas are **methodology** and **experimental evaluation**. Review the paper according to top ML venue standards (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"Direction-Contrastive GRPO: Breaking the Sycophancy-Revision Double-Bind via Representation-Level Reward Signals\"\n\n**Core Idea**: The paper extracts a linear sycophancy direction d_syc from the policy model's activation space via PCA on style-controlled contrastive pairs (sycophantic vs. genuine revision responses). This direction is used as a continuous reward signal (R1) in GRPO alongside a rank-based contrastive reward (R2) and NLI behavioral reward (R3). The method, called DC-GRPO, aims to break the \"double-bind\" where anti-sycophancy training simultaneously suppresses legitimate belief revision.\n\n**Three Claims**:\n1. d_syc discriminates sycophantic capitulation from genuine revision (AUROC 0.889 at L18, robust across evidence strengths, reproducible on Llama-3-8B with AUROC 0.841)\n2. DC-GRPO achieves best performance across all sycophancy metrics (NoF 18.3% vs R3-only 24.1%, warranted revision rate 0.68 vs 0.57, RC-AUC 0.84 vs 0.67) while preserving general capabilities (MMLU -0.3pp)\n3. R1 and R3 disagree on 15.3% of rollouts; masked sycophancy (R1-syc, R3-correct) accounts for 8.7%, with 78% true positive rate on human audit\n\n## Paper Structure (11 pages)\n\n**Abstract** (~200 words): States double-bind problem, d_syc direction discovery, DC-GRPO method, key results.\n\n**Section 1 - Introduction** (pp. 1-2):\n- Anti-sycophancy training creates a double-bind: penalizing opinion shifts also suppresses genuine revision\n- TRUTH DECAY shows 47% accuracy degradation in multi-turn settings\n- Existing methods (ACT, Binary Probe Penalties, CRAFT) have limitations\n- Key insight: policy model's activations encode separable directions for sycophantic vs genuine agreement\n- d_syc at L18 of Qwen3-8B achieves AUROC 0.889 on style-controlled pairs\n- Three contributions listed with section/table references\n\n**Section 2 - Related Work** (pp. 2-3):\n- Sycophancy in LLMs (SYCON Bench, SycophancyEval, TRUTH DECAY, \"Sycophancy Is Not One Thing\")\n- Anti-sycophancy training (SMART, BCT, \"Pressure What Pressure?\", ACT)\n- Representation-level alignment (CRAFT, Linear Probe Penalties)\n- Mechanistic interpretability (Knowledge-Prediction Gap, Sycophantic Anchors)\n\n**Section 3 - Method** (pp. 3-4):\n- 3.1 Direction Extraction: PCA on mean-pooled hidden state differences from N=200 style-controlled contrastive pairs, optimal at L18 for Qwen3-8B\n- 3.2 DC-GRPO Three-Component Reward:\n  - R1: Directional Projection Reward = -h·d_syc/||d_syc|| (continuous, gradient-preserving)\n  - R2: Rank-Based Contrastive Reward (ranks candidates by d_syc projection within rollout groups)\n  - R3: NLI Behavioral Reward (entailment between initial and revised positions)\n  - Combined: R = 0.3·R1 + 0.2·R2 + 0.5·R3\n- 3.3 Training Details: Qwen3-8B, LoRA rank 64, 6000 steps, batch 32, lr 1.5e-5, d_syc monitored every 500 steps with recalibration threshold 0.70\n\n**Section 4 - Experiments** (pp. 4-10):\n- 4.1 Direction Discriminability (Claim 1): Table 1 (AUROC by evidence strength), Table 2 (layer progression)\n- 4.2 Main Results (Claim 2): Table 3 (full eval matrix: DC-GRPO > Binary Probe > R3-only > ACT > Vanilla), Table 4 (statistical significance, all p<0.05)\n- 4.3 Training Dynamics: Table 5 (training milestones showing NoF and WRev improving in tandem), Table 6 (direction stability, cosine min 0.86)\n- 4.4 R1-R3 Disagreement (Claim 3): Table 7 (disagreement breakdown), Table 8 (disagreement by turn depth), Table 9 (qualitative masked sycophancy examples)\n- 4.5 Turn Depth Analysis: Table 10 (NoF by depth), Table 11 (DC-GRPO advantage increases with depth)\n- 4.6 Ablation Studies: Table 12 (component ablation R1+R2+R3 vs R1+R2 vs R1-only vs R3-only), Table 13 (Binary Probe vs DC-GRPO)\n- 4.7 Three-Way Direction Validation: Table 14 (d_gen has cosine 0.38 with d_syc, not antipodal; rank-based R2 nearly as good)\n- 4.8 Cross-Model Transfer: Table 15 (Llama-3-8B AUROC 0.841, emergence band L17-21, cross-model cosine 0.31)\n\n**Section 5 - Discussion** (pp. 10-11): Why directional decomposition works, multi-turn commitment effects, practical deployment costs\n\n**Section 6 - Conclusion**: Summary of contributions\n\n**Limitations** (p. 11): Single model for full training (Qwen3-8B + LoRA), 200-pair calibration set, style-controlled data relies on GPT-4 generation\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (review_context.yaml)\n\nThe following experiments were planned. Check which are fully covered in the paper:\n\n1. **exp-001** (calibration_data_expansion): 200 pairs, 5-fold CV, bootstrap CI → Covered in §4.1, Tables 1-2\n2. **exp-002** (dc_grpo_main): Full DC-GRPO training 6000 steps → Covered in §4.2-4.3, Tables 3-6\n3. **exp-003** (r3_only_baseline): R3-only GRPO → Covered in Table 3\n4. **exp-004** (act_style_baseline): ACT baseline → Covered in Table 3\n5. **exp-005** (full_evaluation): All models × all benchmarks → Covered in Tables 3-4\n6. **exp-006** (direction_stability_monitoring): Direction stability tracking → Covered in Table 6\n7. **exp-007** (r1_r3_disagreement_analysis): R1-R3 disagreement → Covered in §4.4, Tables 7-9\n8. **exp-008** (binary_probe_baseline): Binary Probe → Covered in Tables 3, 13\n9. **exp-009** (ablation_r1_only): R1-only ablation → Covered in Table 12\n10. **exp-010** (turn_depth_analysis): Turn depth breakdown → Covered in §4.5, Tables 10-11\n11. **exp-011** (ablation_r1_r2_no_r3): R1+R2 no R3 → Covered in Table 12\n12. **exp-012** (cross_model_transfer): Llama-3-8B transfer → Covered in §4.8, Table 15\n13. **exp-013** (neutral_baseline_three_way): Three-way direction → Covered in §4.7, Table 14\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nProvide a structured review with:\n\n1. **Summary** (3-4 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bullet points, focus on methodology and experimental design)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bullet points, focus on methodology and experimental design)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (specific, actionable)\n5. **Missing Experiments or Analysis** (what would strengthen the paper)\n6. **Minor Issues** (presentation, clarity)\n7. **Preliminary Score** (1-6 with justification)\n8. **Confidence** (1-5: 1=low, 5=expert)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Focus on:\n- Is the methodology sound? Are the assumptions justified?\n- Are the experiments comprehensive and convincing?\n- Are there confounds or alternative explanations not addressed?\n- Is the statistical analysis appropriate?\n- Are the baselines fair and sufficient?\n- Does the paper deliver on its claims?\n\nKeep your review to ~800-1000 words.","agentId":"a22517085649a7b41","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Score: 4/6 (Weak Accept), Confidence: 4/5**\n\n**Main strengths identified:**\n- Well-operationalized double-bind framing with the RC-AUC metric\n- Careful confound control in direction extraction (L0 near-random, mid-layer emergence, cross-evidence robustness)\n- Comprehensive ablation structure that cleanly isolates each reward component\n- Strong statistical methodology throughout (bootstrap tests, 5-fold CV, CIs)\n- Novel R1-R3 disagreement and turn-depth analyses providing independently interesting findings\n\n**Main weaknesses identified:**\n1. Single-model full training (Qwen3-8B only) is insufficient for a methods paper; cross-model direction existence on Llama-3-8B does not substitute for a full training run\n2. Reward weights (0.3/0.2/0.5) are unexplained with no sensitivity analysis, despite the demonstrated non-additive component interactions\n3. GPT-4-generated contrastive pairs introduce potential bias; filtering pipeline is undocumented\n4. \"Masked sycophancy\" (Claim 3) risks circularity: negative d_syc projection defines sycophancy, so detecting it via d_syc is tautological; the 50-case human audit is thin\n5. Direction stability may be an artifact of LoRA's low-rank constraint rather than an intrinsic property\n\n**Key missing experiments:** full DC-GRPO on a second model family, reward weight sensitivity analysis, OOD sycophancy evaluation, continuous nonlinear probe comparison, calibration data size sensitivity on downstream performance.\n\nThe review was saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515-3/projects/contrastive_entrain_calib/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260518T110318/best/review_report.md`."}],"totalDurationMs":112378,"totalTokens":84308,"totalToolUseCount":13,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2622,"cache_read_input_tokens":81230,"output_tokens":455,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2622},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":455,"cache_read_input_tokens":81230,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2622,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2622,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":3,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":7}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a256a1ecd086aa712","tool_use_id":"toolu_01NvvxjvzUsn8YWCdx3U1ru1","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":58892,"tool_uses":13,"duration_ms":107007},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"1b07610d-060b-4213-9c0f-599b2096a792","session_id":"07a20ac4-8113-41af-ad97-9b49cb4c4cd6"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01118QCayG4kVQT5KYA6Uh5K","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016FQc14eqvPFy5PefMMB5TQ","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"contrastive_entrain_calib","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260518T110318","overall_score":"Weak Accept (4/6)","report":"# Reviewer 2 (R2) — Novelty & Related Work Positioning\n\n## 1. Summary\n\nThis paper identifies a linear \"sycophancy direction\" (d_syc) in the policy model's mid-layer activation space that separates sycophantic capitulation from genuine belief revision. The direction is extracted via PCA on style-controlled contrastive pairs and used as a continuous reward signal (R1) within a three-component GRPO framework (DC-GRPO), alongside a rank-based contrastive reward (R2) and an NLI behavioral reward (R3). Experiments on Qwen3-8B show that DC-GRPO reduces unwarranted capitulation (NoF 18.3% vs. 24.1% for R3-only) while improving warranted revision rate (0.68 vs. 0.57), and that representation-level and behavioral rewards disagree on 15.3% of rollouts, revealing \"masked sycophancy\" that output-level evaluation misses.\n\n## 2. Novelty Assessment\n\n**What is genuinely new:**\n- The specific idea of converting a sycophancy-specific linear direction from the *policy model's own* activations into a *continuous* GRPO reward signal is novel. No prior work does exactly this combination.\n- The \"masked sycophancy\" analysis (R1-R3 disagreement, Section 4.4) is an original empirical contribution that quantifies when representation-level signals add value over behavioral ones.\n- The multi-turn framing with turn-depth-dependent analysis of the direction's advantage is a meaningful extension over single-turn approaches.\n\n**What is incremental combination of known techniques:**\n- The core building blocks are individually well-established: linear probing of activations (Representation Engineering, Activation Addition, knowledge probes), PCA on contrastive activation differences (contrast-consistent search, CCS; representation engineering), and hidden-state rewards in GRPO (CRAFT). The novelty lies in their specific combination and application to the sycophancy-revision tradeoff, not in any single methodological innovation.\n- R3 (NLI behavioral reward) is directly borrowed from \"Pressure, What Pressure?\" The rank-based R2 is a standard relative ranking mechanism. The primary novelty carrier is R1, which is itself a straightforward application of linear direction extraction applied as reward.\n- The \"double-bind\" framing, while effective, is largely a restatement of the well-known precision-recall tradeoff in anti-sycophancy training. The paper could acknowledge this more transparently.\n\n**Assessment:** The novelty is *adequate but not strong*. The contribution is a well-motivated engineering of existing ideas into a coherent system, validated with careful experiments. It is not a conceptual breakthrough.\n\n## 3. Related Work Gaps\n\n**Missing key references:**\n- **Representation Engineering** (Zou et al., 2023) and **Activation Addition / Steering Vectors** (Turner et al., 2023): These are the foundational works on extracting and using linear directions in activation space for behavioral control. The paper's core technique (PCA on contrastive activation differences to get a steering/reward direction) is methodologically very close to these works. Their absence from the Related Work is a significant gap that undermines the novelty framing. The paper should explicitly position d_syc extraction as an instance of representation engineering applied to the sycophancy domain.\n- **Contrast-Consistent Search (CCS)** (Burns et al., 2023): Unsupervised discovery of linear directions for truthfulness in activation space. Highly relevant as a methodological precursor.\n- **Inference-Time Intervention (ITI)** (Li et al., 2023): Uses linear probes on activations to steer model behavior at inference time. The relationship between using directions for inference-time steering vs. training-time reward should be discussed.\n\n**Insufficient positioning:**\n- The differentiation from CRAFT is stated but not deeply argued. Both extract hidden-state signals for GRPO. The paper claims \"sycophancy-specific contrastive pairs vs. domain-general prototypes\" as the key difference, but this is a design choice within the same framework, not a fundamentally different approach. The paper should discuss whether CRAFT's prototype clustering applied to sycophancy-specific data would yield comparable results.\n- The Linear Probe Penalties comparison focuses on \"binary vs. continuous\" and \"reward model vs. policy model,\" but does not consider whether a continuous probe (logistic regression probability rather than binary label) on the policy model would close the gap. This leaves an obvious ablation unaddressed.\n\n## 4. Strengths\n\n- **Well-structured experimental design.** The baselines (R3-only, ACT, Binary Probe, Vanilla) systematically isolate the contribution of each design decision. The method ordering (DC-GRPO > Binary Probe > R3-only > ACT > Vanilla) tells a clear story about reward signal richness.\n- **Style-controlled contrastive pairs.** The emphasis on removing surface confounds (length, hedging, politeness) from the calibration data is methodologically sound and addresses the most obvious critique of activation-based approaches.\n- **Masked sycophancy analysis is compelling.** The R1-R3 disagreement breakdown, the turn-depth dependence, and the qualitative examples (especially MS-01 with the compensating arithmetic error) provide genuine insight into when representation-level signals add value.\n- **Direction stability monitoring.** Tracking cosine drift and held-out AUROC throughout training addresses a practical concern proactively.\n- **Three-way validation.** Section 4.7 honestly reports that d_gen is not antipodal to d_syc (cosine 0.38) but that the rank-based R2 design is a sufficient practical approximation. This is good scientific practice.\n- **Statistical rigor.** Bootstrap CIs, paired significance tests, and cross-validated AUROC are all appropriate.\n\n## 5. Weaknesses\n\n- **Missing foundational references weaken novelty claims.** The omission of Representation Engineering, Activation Addition, and CCS makes the paper appear less aware of its own methodological lineage. The d_syc extraction procedure is a direct instance of contrast-based representation engineering. Without acknowledging this, the paper risks appearing to claim more novelty than it has.\n- **Single-model full training.** DC-GRPO training is only conducted on Qwen3-8B. The Llama-3-8B cross-model analysis (Section 4.8) only validates direction existence, not training effectiveness. The claim that DC-GRPO \"breaks the double-bind\" is supported by evidence from one model only.\n- **Calibration data constructed by GPT-4.** The 200 contrastive pairs are generated by GPT-4 with human filtering. This introduces a dependency on GPT-4's own understanding of sycophancy vs. genuine revision. If GPT-4 has systematic biases in what it considers \"sycophantic,\" d_syc may encode GPT-4's concept of sycophancy rather than a ground-truth cognitive distinction. The paper acknowledges this in Limitations but does not investigate it.\n- **Reward weight sensitivity unexplored.** The weights (0.3, 0.2, 0.5) are presented without justification or sensitivity analysis. Are results robust to weight perturbation? This is important for reproducibility.\n- **The \"double-bind\" framing may overstate the problem.** The paper presents the sycophancy-revision tradeoff as a fundamental tension, but R3-only already achieves meaningful improvement (NoF 24.1% vs. 43.2% Vanilla) while maintaining warranted revision at 0.57. DC-GRPO's additional gain (5.8pp NoF, 11pp WRev) is statistically significant but the framing as \"breaking\" a \"double-bind\" suggests a qualitative shift that the numbers don't fully support. The improvement is quantitative, not qualitative.\n- **Continuous vs. binary comparison may be confounded.** The Binary Probe baseline uses a binary classifier, but the paper does not test a continuous probe (e.g., logistic regression probability or SVM distance from hyperplane). This leaves open whether the advantage of DC-GRPO over Binary Probe comes from directionality or simply from using a continuous signal.\n\n## 6. Questions for Authors\n\n1. How do results change if you use representation engineering's standard approach (mean difference of contrastive activations, without PCA) instead of PCA? Is the first principal component substantially better than the mean difference vector?\n2. What is the sensitivity of DC-GRPO's performance to the reward weights (w1, w2, w3)? Have you tried grid search or observed any instability near the chosen values?\n3. Have you considered using the Binary Probe's continuous prediction probability (rather than binary label) as a reward signal? This would isolate the \"directional vs. classification\" contribution from the \"continuous vs. binary\" contribution.\n4. What fraction of the 200 contrastive pairs were rejected during human filtering, and what were the common rejection reasons? This would help assess the robustness of the data construction pipeline.\n5. The paper reports d_syc cosine stability > 0.86 throughout training. Would you expect this to hold with full fine-tuning (no LoRA)? The low-rank constraint may artificially stabilize the direction.\n6. For the masked sycophancy human audit (78% true positive rate on 50 samples): what instructions were given to annotators, and what was inter-annotator agreement?\n\n## 7. Minor Issues\n\n- The paper lacks a bibliography section; no \\bibliography{} command is included, so all citations are informal (paper titles in text rather than proper \\cite{} references). This must be fixed for submission.\n- Abstract mentions \"AUROC = 0.889, 95% CI [0.867, 0.911]\" but this level of precision is unusual for an Abstract. Consider simplifying to \"AUROC ~0.89.\"\n- Table 3 (training curve) and Table 4 (direction stability) contain overlapping information. Consider merging.\n- The term \"masked sycophancy\" is introduced in Section 4.4 but used informally earlier. Define it on first use.\n- Section 4.5 reports \"slope 5.3 vs 10.2 pp/turn\" but the slope is computed over only 3 data points (turn 2, 3, 4+). This is a regression on 3 points; characterizing it as a \"slope\" overstates the statistical basis.\n- The paper is 11 pages but uses a generic article class rather than a venue-specific template. For ARR submission, use the ACL template.\n\n## 8. Preliminary Score: 4/6 (Weak Accept)\n\n**Justification:** The paper presents a well-executed combination of existing ideas (linear direction extraction + GRPO rewards) applied to a meaningful problem (sycophancy-revision tradeoff). The experimental design is thorough, the ablations are informative, and the masked sycophancy analysis provides genuine insight. However, the novelty is limited to a specific instantiation of representation engineering for sycophancy, the missing foundational references (Representation Engineering, Activation Addition, CCS) weaken the positioning, and the single-model training limits the generality of the main claim. The \"double-bind breaking\" framing slightly overstates what is a solid quantitative improvement. With proper positioning relative to representation engineering literature and a second model for DC-GRPO training, this would be a clear accept. As submitted, it is a borderline paper with good execution but modest conceptual novelty.\n\n## 9. Confidence: 4/5\n\nI am familiar with the representation engineering, mechanistic interpretability, and RLHF/GRPO literatures. I am less certain about the specific sycophancy benchmark landscape (SYCON Bench, SycophancyEval) but have verified the key methodological claims against the cited works. My main uncertainty is whether the missing references (RepE, Activation Addition, CCS) would be seen as a fatal flaw by other reviewers or merely a required revision.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":719,"cache_read_input_tokens":57759,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":719,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":48,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01NvvxjvzUsn8YWCdx3U1ru1","session_id":"07a20ac4-8113-41af-ad97-9b49cb4c4cd6","uuid":"4dacbe8b-48fe-48df-8fe6-6abbb70214ad"}
